nd select the PGX assay  button or the preset from the instrument  screen to start the run and apply the  preset parameters  they would then export the data to a USB  or a network drive for analysis once the  run is completed  um they would then take the um the  generic sample and acid annotations on  the on the file and up to update them  accordingly to be  um run specific so you'd have your  specific samples that you want to run in  any customization that you've done to  the on the assay side would be annotated  by the user and then they open the files  and the analysis software  and then from there these the system  takes over so we'll then take the the  system creates two chip run files from  the exported data one is for the Snips  and one is for the cmds and these are  then combined with the annotations and  the assay names  um that are in the files provided by the  user  and then they would apply the system the  system then it applies the preset  parameters for analysis and Reporting  and then determines all the snip CMV and  star allele calls and then it finally  issues the report with all this  information for review by the by the um  the lab personnel  this just shows you an example of this  snip assay setup file that we provide it  comes as a preset and then if somebody's  trying to customize the assay they could  they can make modifications as needed  and that's where we would want to work  with you to make sure that it's being  done smoothly and understand and  appreciate what experience you're having  trying to implement this as it's been  designed that way if there's needs to  make refinements later we can look into  doing so  we use a a truth table for our star  allele calls so we take the snip output  data from the system and compare it to a  preset truth table and here's an example  just showing the table on the lower  right it shows you examples of star  alleles for the different um for sip2d6  as an example and if you're trying in  this case to lo